<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>7.11.4: Using the Vertex and Axis of Symmetry of Quadratics</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01930</md:content-id>
    <md:title>7.11.4: Using the Vertex and Axis of Symmetry of Quadratics</md:title>
    <md:uuid>a6c1c509-dc95-4f31-a3f1-500426620bbb</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Activity</title>
<para>While answering the questions below, plot each point on a coordinate plane with grid paper by hand. Do not use technology.</para>
<para>For questions 1 – 4, use the the function \(f(x)=(x-2)(x+4).\)</para>
<!--Q#1-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> Find the \(x\)-coordinates of the \(x\)-intercepts of \(f(x)\). </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>\(x=-4\) and \(x =2\)</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->
<!--Q#2-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> Find the coordinate of the vertex. </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>\((-1, -5)\)</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->
<!--Q#3-->
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para> On your graph, sketch a quadratic through the key features on your graph and draw a vertical line through the vertex using a different color.  </para>
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  <para><figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/7c569cfd15ffb1afb73d2259d8906b0d96741eb8"/></media></figure></para>
</solution></exercise>
</example><!--Interaction End -->
<!--Q#4-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> What is the equation of the vertical line that goes through the vertex? </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>\(x=-1\)</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->
<!--Q#5-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> What does the vertical line that goes through the vertex do to the parabola? </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>Your answer may vary, but here is a sample. The vertical line \(x= -1\) cuts the parabola in half and creates two symmetrical parts.</para>
    <para>This imaginary vertical line that goes through the vertex and cuts the parabola in half is called the <term data-schema-version="1.0" data-store="glossary-tooltip">axis of symmetry</term>.</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->
<!--Q#6-->
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para> On your graph, label your \(x\)-intercepts, vertex, and axis of symmetry. Identify your vertex as a maximum or minimum. </para>
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  <para><figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/2355010bb11c4a259c69fb1b8f261912d62bbcfb"/></media></figure></para>
</solution></exercise>
</example><!--Interaction End -->
<!--Q#7-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> Given a vertex of \((2, -4)\) and a point \((1, 3)\), what is another point on the quadratic? </para>
    <para>(Hint: It is helpful to draw a picture and use the axis of symmetry).</para>
  </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>Your answer may vary, but here is a sample. </para>
    <para><figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/d62313b400bbc8ca6fcb49bcdae93601a6a71a5f"/></media></figure></para>
    <para>Since \((-2, 4)\) is the vertex, then \(x=-2\) is the axis of symmetry. If \((1, 3)\) is 3 units away from the axis of symmetry, there is another point 3 units to the left of the axis of symmetry. This is the point \((-5, 3)\).</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->
</section>
